cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use pyfixest in your research, please cite it as below,
  and also cite fixest (Bergé, Butts & McDermott, 2026), whose design,
  interface, and defaults pyfixest reimplements in Python.
title: "pyfixest: Fast high-dimensional fixed effect estimation in Python"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "The PyFixest Authors"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.15814089
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.15814089
    description: Concept DOI (always resolves to the latest version)
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.18674882
    description: DOI for version v0.50.0alpha1
version: v0.50.0alpha1
date-released: "2026-02-17"
repository-code: 'https://github.com/py-econometrics/pyfixest'
url: 'https://pyfixest.org/'
abstract: "A Python package for fast high-dimensional fixed effect estimation, inspired by the syntax of the popular R package `fixest`. It provides a familiar and intuitive interface for economists and other social scientists to estimate complex regression models with multiple fixed effects efficiently."
keywords:
  - 'fixed effects'
  - 'panel data'
  - 'econometrics'
  - 'statistics'
  - 'causal inference'
  - 'regression'
  - 'python'
license: MIT
references:
  - type: article
    title: "fixest: A fast and feature-rich framework for econometric estimations in R"
    authors:
      - family-names: Bergé
        given-names: Laurent R
      - family-names: Butts
        given-names: Kyle
      - family-names: McDermott
        given-names: Grant
    year: 2026
    journal: "arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.21749"
    notes: >-
      Please cite fixest alongside pyfixest. PyFixest reimplements
      fixest's design, interface, and defaults in Python.
